  "brief_description": "This bold picture is a good example of the portrait style of Venetian painter Alvise Vivarini. He was well known for describing his sitters' individual features in detail -- here he has made sure to include the man's wrinkles and the dark circles under his eyes. The man's confident expression was the result of a lot of experimentation: technical evidence shows that Alvise repositioned the pupils several times.\n\nAlvise borrowed a number of elements from Antonello da Messina's portrait style, which was hugely popular in Venice at around this time. These included strong lighting, a dark background and the three-quarter angle of the sitter's head and shoulders. He has also added the man's hand, behind a marble ledge, details that came from fashionable Netherlandish painting. These make the portrait livelier and give the impression of a separate space behind the ledge.",
